WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.880
We have been given five digits one, two, three, four, and five.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.280
And along with that we have this question.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.400
Using these five digits,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.860
how many three-digit odd numbers can be formed?

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.120
First case when repetition is not allowed.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.640
And second when repetition is allowed.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.000
By repetition not being allowed,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.020
they mean we cannot consider numbers like,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.540
131 or 224 which have repeated digits.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.960
Let's understand the first part.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.800
Read the question well.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:44.660
How many three-digit odd numbers? Odd is the key word here.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.960
As we have been asked for three-digit numbers,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.960
let's make three slots.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.120
What do we mean by odd?

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.420
A number is odd, if it is not divisible by 2.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.940
Is 342 odd? No, it's not odd as it is divisible by 2.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.380
What about 531?

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:11.300
Is this odd? Yes it is. Because it is not divisible by 2.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.980
Similarly, 325 and 243 are odd.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.940
In simple words this three-digit number will be odd,

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.520
if it's unit digit is odd.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.160
So based on the condition we have,

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:32.740
the three-digit number can end with a '1' or end with a '3' or end with a '5'.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.240
Correct!

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.380
These are the only three possibilities here.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.380
Now focus on the first possibility.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.340
The number ends with a '1'.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.120
In how many ways can we fill the first slot or the hundredth slot?

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.800
What do you think?

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.200
The digit 1 is gone.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.660
So the hundredths place can be filled with any one

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:57.800
of the remaining four digits 2, 3, 4 or 5.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.720
4 ways in which it can be filled.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.640
What about the second slot? The digit 1 is gone.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.240
And another digit out of these 4 is gone into the hundredths place.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.200
Only three digits remain.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.700
So the second slot can be filled in three ways.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.080
With 1 in the unit's place,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.360
and as we are filling the first and the second slot we multiply this.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.460
'And' is always multiplication.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.860
Four times three is twelve.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.560
Given these five digits,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.360
there are twelve ways in which we can form a three-digit number

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.580
with one as its units digit.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.360
Are we done?

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:41.060
No! We still have to look at these two cases.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.820
In the second case, the digit 3 is gone.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.660
The first slot can be filled with one or a two or a four or a five.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.380
Four ways.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.400
As two digits are gone the tenth place can be filled in three ways.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.800
Again, as we are filling the first and the second slot,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.520
we have a multiplication sign here.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.960
Given these five digits, there are 12 ways in which we can form

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.460
a three-digit number with 3 as its units digit.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.960
And similarly we get a 12 here as well.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.200
Let's zoom out slightly!

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.760
What do we do with these three numbers?

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.580
Do we add them or multiply them?

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.700
To know the answer just use plain English.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.460
The unit's digit is 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 5?

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.680
Or should we say that the unit's digit can be

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.680
1 or 3 or a 5?

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.680
Of course, it's an 'or'.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.380
Based on the condition given, the unit's digit can be 1 or 3 or 5.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.320
An 'or' implies plus .

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.320
Adding these three we get the answer as 36 ways.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.820
36 ways in which we can form three-digit odd numbers,

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.240
using these five digits.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.700
Now this was slightly long and a more logical way of solving this.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.640
Now that we know the concept.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.420
Let's look at a quicker way to solve it.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.760
We know it is a three-digit number.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.280
What is the condition given to us?

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.320
The condition is that the number should be odd.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.100
Remember a Golden rule.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.880
Whenever we are given a condition, start with it.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.920
As the condition is about the numbers being odd,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.200
we can be sure that it depends on the unit's digit.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.280
So we start off with the unit's digit slot.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.160
In how many ways can we fill this slot ?

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:35.280
It can be filled with a one or a three or a five.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.220
Three ways.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.360
Now we can move on to the other two slots.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.960
One digit is gone. It doesn't matter what digit it is.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.200
If one digit is gone then four remain.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.460
The hundredth place can be filled in four ways.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.100
If the unit's digit is filled with one,

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:59.520
any digit from two, three, four or five can be in the hundredth place.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.660
If the unit's digit is filled with three

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.560
any digit from one to four or five

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.420
can be in the hundredth place.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.900
And when two digits are used for the

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.240
hundredth and the unit's place, just three remain.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.280
The second slot can be filled in three ways.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.960
And we multiply these to get the answer thirty-six ways.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.600
We got the same answer, but a lot quicker.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.740
Some of you would be thinking,

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:30.460
why start with the condition? What if we don't?

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.400
Let's say we decide to start with the hundredth place.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.020
In how many ways can we fill the hundredth place digit?

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.920
We can fill it with any of the five digits.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.420
Five ways.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.480
Having used one digit here,

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.960
the tenth place can be filled in four ways.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.600
And now we come to the unit's place.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.900
In how many ways can we fill the unit's place?

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.460
Can we say three ways as three digits are left?

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:56.860
Maybe not.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:59.380
Let's say we used a one here.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:02.340
We have two, three, four, and five left.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.600
Say I pick a three here.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.560
Now in how many ways can the unit's digit be filled?

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.360
It has to be odd.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.520
The only odd digit that remains is five.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:17.440
It can be filled in just one way and that's five.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.360
Let's take another scenario,

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.780
Say we fill the hundredth place with two and we picked a four in the tens place.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:28.160
Now tell me in how many ways can we fill the unit's digit?

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:32.560
One, three, or five. All will keep the number odd.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:37.520
In this case there are three ways in which the unit's digit can be filled.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:42.440
So we end up with conflicting answers if we don't start with the condition.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:46.880
When forming numbers, remember to start with the condition.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.180
There are 36 ways in which we can form three-digit odd numbers

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.180
using these five digits

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.480
if repetition is not allowed.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.380
And what if repetition is allowed?

00:06:58.760 --> 00:06:59.500
Remember,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.320
life becomes really easy if repetition is allowed.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.260
Let's solve it on a new page.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.320
Using these five digits,

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.840
how many three-digit odd numbers can be formed if repetition is allowed?

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.260
Okay, let's solve the second part.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:20.000
We are talking about three-digit numbers here, and they have to be odd.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:21.900
Let's start with the condition.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:25.020
In how many ways can we fill the unit's digit?

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.120
As the number has to be odd,

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:31.800
we can fill the unit's digit with either a '1' or a ''3 or a '5'.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.000
3 ways in which the unit's digit can be filled.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.240
We are done with the condition.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.640
In how many ways can we fill the hundredth place now?

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.360
What do you think?

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:51.400
As one digit is used here,

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.920
the hundredth place can be filled in four ways. Correct?

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.480
No, it's not.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.020
Don't forget,

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.100
repetition is allowed!

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:03.160
We can use the same digit here as well.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.880
Five ways in which the hundredth place can be filled.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.960
Any one of the five digits,

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.160
and what about the tenth place?

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:16.500
Again it can be filled with any one of the five digits, five ways.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:20.500
As we are filling the first and the second and the third slot,

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.800
we multiply these numbers.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:26.120
We get the answer as 75 ways.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:29.040
There are 75 three-digit odd numbers,

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:34.280
we can form using these five digits if repetition is allowed.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.960
Here's my last question in this session.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:41.660
If repetition is allowed, do we have to start with the condition?

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:44.660
Let's say we start with the hundredth place.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:47.480
It can be filled with any of the five digits.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:51.800
It will be the same with the tenth digit as repetition is allowed.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.820
And the unit's digit again ,can be filled in three ways.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:09:00.260
The answer does not change even if we start with the hundredth place.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:04.920
If repetition is allowed, we don't really have to start with the condition.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.600
Don't stress yourself out by having to remember this.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.100
As a general rule just start with the condition.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:15.340
That's how we deal with numbers with a condition.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.060
We will cover a few more interesting examples

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.220
in the coming sessions.

